  "question": "What brightness temperature would you expect from Arctic snow?",
  "answer": "The expected brightness temperature from Arctic snow, based on the SMRT model with default parameters (37 GHz frequency, 55° incidence angle, 1 m thickness, 300 kg/m³ density, and 265 K temperature), is approximately:\n\n- **Horizontal polarization (tb_h):** 112.4 K  \n- **Vertical polarization (tb_v):** 127.2 K  \n\nThis result aligns with the high-frequency validation case described in the [arctic-snow-emission](arctic-snow-emission#01) study, which evaluated SMRT against airborne radiometry for Arctic snow. The model assumes an exponential microstructure and IBA electromagnetic theory, valid for dry snow below freezing. \n\n[model:smrt@1.5.1]",
